    "summaryThesis": "In moments of strategic crisis, a society lacking predictable institutions and exhausted by chronic stress compensates for its insecurity by regressive defense patterns: the Puer Aeternus syndrome, dependence on a tutelary paternalistic state, and manic defense. These mechanisms fuel the magical thinking of the \"saviour\" and the illusion of isolationist neutrality, leading to the decision-making paralysis of the target state.",
    "paragraphs": [
      "The generalization of collective self-sabotage around decisive electoral moments or regional security crises blocks the strategic decision-making capacity of the target society. At the level of Layer C, this difficulty to assume historical reality and the costs of political maturity is expressed through the psychodynamic pattern of the archetype Puer Aeternus (the eternal child or adolescent) and through the regression towards passive dependence on a tutelary paternalistic state. In accordance with the epistemological delimitation established in the previous chapter (Ryle, 1949), these analytical concepts do not hypothesize autonomous metaphysical entities, but synthesize the psychosocial defense mechanisms through which a community faced with institutional unpredictability (Layer A) and allostatic exhaustion (Layer B) avoids the effort of political maturation. Analyzed by Marie-Louise von Franz (1970/2000) and James Hillman (1979), Puer Aeternus syndrome designates an individual or collective psychic attitude that refuses patient, methodical and limited engagement in the concrete world. Instead of incremental construction, this defensive pattern seeks refuge in a regime of perpetual provisioning, magical thinking, and waiting for a miraculous solution that will instantly solve all difficulties, effortlessly and without compromise.",
      "In Romanian political culture, this reactive pattern of the collective Puer has historically manifested itself through the recurring oscillation between short-lived messianic enthusiasms and prolonged episodes of collective ego deflation, learned helplessness and defensive fatalism. Modern institution building, administration reform, judicial strengthening, scientific education and national defense are slow cumulative processes. They demand fiscal discipline, compliance with procedures, cross-party solidarity and lucid acceptance of geopolitical constraints. For a community whose store of trust and neurocognitive energy has been chronically eroded, this patient institutional work feels like an exhausting and horizonless burden. In its place, the vulnerable collective imaginary looks for compensatory shortcuts: passively waiting for a providential \"savior\", imagined as a charismatic leader above laws and parties, who will \"make order\" overnight. Added to this mirage is the refuge in what Erich Neumann (1949/1954) described as the collective regression to passive dependence on a tutelary paternalistic state. This regression expresses a defensive nostalgia for the dependency state of late communism, in which the totalitarian state, though oppressive and marked by chronic scarcity, spared the individual the anguish of free choice and competition. In the terms of the psychodynamics of groups formulated by Wilfred R. Bion (1961), a collectivity whose institutions no longer provide predictable security regresses from the state of \"work group\" (oriented towards reality testing, rational cooperation and methodical problem solving) into the archaic state of \"basic assumptions\". The three political manifestations of the Romanian crisis correspond exactly to the three basic hypotheses described by Bion: nostalgia for the tutelary paternalistic state expresses the basic hypothesis of dependence (baD), the magical expectation of a charismatic \"savior\" situated above the law expresses the basic hypothesis of messianic pairing (baP), and the paranoid mobilization against some \"external persecutors\" or scapegoats expresses the basic fight-or-flight hypothesis (baF). Moreover, Bion (1961) demonstrates that, while maintaining a \"work group\" requires institutional effort and epistemic maturity, slippage in basic assumptions is instantly triggered by \"valency\" (valency — the pre-rational and involuntary willingness of distressed individuals to merge affectively into the mass) and follows an unstable cyclical dynamic: whereas the magical promise of the messianic savior (baP) or tutelary state (baD) cannot really solve structural problems, the inevitable disappointment of miraculous expectations periodically tips the collectivity into paranoid rage and fight-or-flight (baF) aggression.",
      "This compensatory mechanism provides the key to understanding the attraction that the illusion of isolationist sovereignty and geopolitical \"neutrality\" promoted by Russian cognitive warfare vectors has on certain segments of the population. In a regional context dominated by the Russian Federation's war of aggression against Ukraine and the direct threat to the security of the Black Sea basin, revisionist propaganda launches a seductive narrative. According to her, Romania could achieve \"perpetual peace\", cheap gas and autarkic prosperity if it stopped supporting the aggressed neighboring state, refused military endowment spending and withdrew into a \"sovereign neutrality\" between East and West. Strategically and historically, this proposition is a dangerous fiction. The entire modern history of Central and Eastern Europe demonstrates that, in the proximity of a revisionist empire, unarmed neutrality and isolation from Western alliances never guarantee peace. On the contrary, they represent the sure prelude to territorial dismemberment and occupation, as demonstrated by the tragedy of 1940 for Romania, Poland and the Baltic states or the invasion of neutral Ukraine in 2014 (Snyder, 2018). However, for a collective consciousness taking refuge in the defensive Puer Aeternus pattern, the isolationist illusion remains extremely attractive. It allows the exhausted citizenry to deny the existence of real danger—through the mechanism of manic defense and reality denial (Klein, 1946/1975)—and to believe that war can be avoided simply by moral and diplomatic capitulation to the aggressor.",
      "The immediate political effect of this convergence of regressive mechanisms in Layer C, the slide into Quadrant III of Cipolla's matrix, and the hijacking of the OODA loop is the institutional paralysis of the target state. When the electorate and the political class are split into irreconcilable camps captive to the logic of the binary semiosis of annihilation, the state's ability to make coherent strategic decisions in a timely manner is blocked. The nation's administrative, budgetary and diplomatic energies are consumed in sterile internal conflicts, in repeated constitutional crises and in challenging the legitimacy of democratic processes. This blockage occurs exactly in the windows of time when the European security architecture requires a predictable, firm and anticipatory Romanian strategic conduct. Decisional paralysis achieved without firing a single shot thus constitutes the ultimate goal of cognitive warfare in the sixth operational domain."
    ],
